      "If a client holds a meaningful position in bitcoin, they hold something with two properties no other asset on their balance sheet has: it is liquid twenty-four hours a day, and selling it triggers a capital gain immediately with no deferral mechanism comparable to a 1031 exchange. That combination makes it unusually well suited to collateralised borrowing and unusually badly suited to funding a purchase by sale.",
      "The mechanic is simple. You transfer the asset to a lender, it is held in custody or in a shared-key vault, and you receive dollars or stablecoins. You pay interest. When you repay, you get the asset back. No credit is pulled at any lender in this category — the collateral is the underwriting — and funding typically completes in one to two business days, which is faster than any real-estate-secured product exists.",
      "Rates in 2026 span a wide range, and the range is not noise. Arch Lending publishes from 7.25% APR. Ledn publishes 9.25% to 11.49%, with the rate starting at the top and falling as the loan gets larger. SALT sits around 14.5%. Unchained is around 16.6%. A four-point spread between the cheapest and the most expensive of the serious lenders looks like a straightforward shopping exercise, and it is not — the spread is buying different things, and which one you want depends entirely on how much you have at stake.",
      "What the spread buys is custody risk. This category has a history. Several large crypto lenders failed in 2022 and took customer collateral with them, and every survivor now competes on the answer to one question: can you lend out my coins? Ledn's answer is that collateral sits in cold storage with BitGo Trust, ring-fenced from its funding partners, that neither Ledn nor any financing vehicle has the right to lend it, and that it publishes monthly Proof-of-Reserves attestations and holds SOC 2. Arch custodies with Anchorage Digital, a qualified custodian, with zero rehypothecation. Unchained does something structurally different: the collateral sits in a two-of-three multisig vault where the borrower holds one key, Unchained holds one, and a third-party key agent holds the third. Unchained cannot rehypothecate your bitcoin because it does not hold enough keys to move it. That is not a promise, it is arithmetic, and it is what the extra five points over Arch is buying.",
      "For contrast, YouHodler publishes up to 90% loan-to-value at up to 27%, on 30, 60 or 180 day terms, and its collateral is lent out. Every one of those numbers is a warning rather than a feature. Ninety percent LTV on an asset that has repeatedly fallen fifty percent in a quarter is a liquidation with a date not yet assigned, and rehypothecated collateral stacks counterparty risk on top of price risk. It is listed on this page for completeness and as a boundary marker.",
      "Loan-to-value is where the real decision sits. Ledn lends at 50%. Arch goes to 60%. SALT goes to 70%. Coinbase Borrow stops at 40%. These are not competing offers so much as competing risk postures, and the borrower's choice of LTV matters more than the lender's rate. Ledn publishes its liquidation ladder explicitly: alerts at 70% and 75% LTV, automatic partial liquidation beginning at 80%. Work that backwards. A loan opened at 50% LTV hits the 80% liquidation threshold when the collateral falls by about 37.5%. A loan opened at 70% LTV hits it when the collateral falls by about 12.5%. Bitcoin has fallen more than twelve percent in a week many times. Opening at the maximum available LTV is the most common way this route goes wrong, and it is entirely self-inflicted.",
      "The tax treatment is the reason this exists at all. Borrowing against an appreciated asset is not a taxable event. Selling $500,000 of bitcoin with a $100,000 basis realises $400,000 of long-term gain and, at a 23.8% federal rate including the net investment income tax, costs roughly $95,000 before any state tax. Borrowing $250,000 against the same position at 9.25% costs about $23,000 a year. The break-even against simply selling is somewhere past four years, and that comparison is the entire investment case. It is also why this route pairs naturally with a property purchase you intend to hold.",
      "On using the proceeds for real estate: SALT states plainly that funds can be used for essentially any personal or business purpose, which includes property. The practical friction is that some lenders fund in USDC rather than dollars — Coinbase Borrow does — which means a conversion step, an exchange, a banking rail and a day or two before the money is wirable to a title company. Plan for that in a purchase timeline rather than discovering it during a contingency period.",
      "Now the other direction, because the question is usually asked both ways. Stablecoins can be lent out for yield rather than borrowed against. Ledn publishes 6.5% to 8.5% APY on USDT and USDC, tiered so larger balances earn more. On-chain, Aave's USDC vaults ranged from 0.34% to 4.79% on a seven-day average in September 2026, with the deepest pool — Aave v3 USDC on Ethereum, $2.3 billion of total value locked — paying 3.55%. The general market spans roughly 3% to 15% APY, and the spread maps almost perfectly onto risk: higher yields reflect weaker collateral structures, lockups, or token incentives that may not persist.",
      "The honest framing for a rental-property owner is this. Stablecoin yield at 6.5% to 8.5% is not obviously better than the alternatives available to someone who owns real estate — it is uninsured, it carries smart-contract or counterparty risk depending on venue, the peg itself is a risk however unlikely, and the tax treatment is ordinary income. Against a mortgage costing 7.5%, paying down the mortgage is a risk-free 7.5% and lending stablecoins at 8% is a risky 8%. Those are not the same trade, and anyone presenting them as comparable is selling something.",
      "Where this genuinely shines is the specific case: a client with a large, low-basis crypto position who needs property capital and does not want to sell. For that person, at a conservative LTV, with a lender whose custody they have actually investigated, it is one of the cheapest and fastest sources of capital available anywhere on this site. For everyone else it is an interesting product looking for a problem they do not have.",
