    family: "private-capital", depth: "structured",
    collateral: "Conventional real property security, but underwritten with community impact weighed alongside credit.",
    body: ["Small-balance property is where conventional lending stops being economic. A $90,000 rental generates too little origination revenue to justify a bank's underwriting cost, so the bank declines — not because the deal is bad but because the deal is small. That is a structural gap, and CDFIs exist to fill it.", "A Community Development Financial Institution is certified by the US Treasury's CDFI Fund and lends into markets and deal sizes conventional lenders decline. Underwriting weighs community impact alongside credit, which means a deal that fails a bank's box on a technicality can still work if the impact case is real.", "Rates are frequently below market and almost always far below private money, which makes this the cheapest capital available for exactly the deals that otherwise attract the most expensive. The people making the decisions are reachable and the underwriting is genuinely flexible rather than flexible in the marketing sense.", "The constraints are geographic and mandate-based. A CDFI lends within its own defined service area and nowhere else, so the relevant question is not which CDFI is best but which ones lend where you are buying. Mission alignment also matters: a purely opportunistic investor may simply not fit, and it is better to understand that early than to spend weeks discovering it.", "Timelines are slower than private money and sometimes slower than a bank. Loan sizes cap well below what a portfolio investor eventually needs. But the relationship compounds — the second loan is easier than the first — and for an investor working in a low-to-moderate income market these are frequently the best terms available anywhere.", "Start at the CDFI Fund's own searchable list of certified institutions rather than with a broker who claims certification. Certification is a checkable fact and the Fund's list settles it.",
      "It is worth understanding why the terms are what they are, because it changes how you approach the conversation. A CDFI is capitalised in part through federal awards and mission-aligned investors who accept a below-market return in exchange for measurable community outcomes. That funding structure is what lets the institution price below a bank on a deal a bank would decline — but it also means the institution has to be able to report on the outcome. An applicant who can articulate what the project does for the neighbourhood, in terms the institution can put in a report, is a materially stronger applicant than one who arrives with only a spreadsheet.",
      "That is not a rhetorical exercise. Affordable units preserved, a vacant property returned to use, jobs created, a building brought up to code in a neighbourhood losing housing stock — these are the things the institution is funded to produce. An investor whose deal genuinely does one of them and says so plainly will often find underwriting flexibility that no amount of credit strength would have purchased at a bank.",
      "The relationship dimension is also real and it is the part most investors underestimate. Bank lending is transactional and the second loan is underwritten exactly like the first. CDFI lending is relational: a borrower who performs, reports accurately and stays in the market becomes a known quantity, and known quantities get faster decisions and larger facilities. For an investor committed to a specific market rather than opportunistically moving between them, that compounding is worth more over a decade than the initial rate advantage."],
    underwritingMechanics: ["Certification is verifiable at cdfifund.gov. A claim of CDFI status is not the same as certification.", "Service area is the first filter: a CDFI lends in its territory only.", "Community impact is weighed alongside conventional credit metrics.", "Loan sizes are typically small-balance, which is precisely where conventional lending is uneconomic.", "Technical assistance frequently accompanies the capital."],
