    questions: [{ question: "Can you use an SBA loan to buy a building?", answer: "Yes, if your own business will occupy it. The 504 programme pairs a bank first mortgage with a Certified Development Company second and is designed for exactly this. Owner-occupancy is a hard requirement — the business must occupy a defined majority of the space — so it cannot be used for a pure investment property." },{ question: "What down payment does an SBA 504 loan require?", answer: "Far below conventional commercial lending, which is what makes the purchase reachable for a practice also funding equipment and payroll. Confirm the current figure on the SBA programme page, as the structure varies with the property type and whether the business is a start-up." },{ question: "Is SBA 504 or 7(a) better for real estate?", answer: "504 is built for long-term fixed assets and its CDC second carries a long-dated fixed rate that is hard to find elsewhere. 7(a) is broader and can fold working capital in alongside the property. If the purchase is purely the building and you want rate certainty for decades, 504 is usually the better structure." },],
